{#-
  Strict-Transport-Security, and why it says what it says (issue 060).

  Every server below is TLS with a Let's Encrypt certificate and port 80 does
  nothing but redirect, so the site is already HTTPS-only -- but a browser only
  learns that by being redirected, one request at a time, for ever. Issue 059
  emptied that first cleartext request of cookies; the header is what stops it
  being made. It goes on the TLS server only: a browser is required to ignore
  Strict-Transport-Security on a cleartext response, and anybody able to answer
  port 80 instead of nginx could send it just as easily.

  It is deliberately staged, and the deployed configuration says which
  stage it is on, because the header is a promise with a duration and cannot be
  withdrawn faster than it expires. What it makes load-bearing is the certbot
  renewal cron configured in vars/main.yml: root, 03:30, `certbot renew` with
  nginx stopped and started around it, whose failures go to cron's mail on the
  host and are read by nobody. Today an expired certificate is a warning a
  visitor can click through; under a year-long max-age it is a page no browser
  will open. So stage 1 is 300 seconds -- long enough for one deploy to prove
  all three names are genuinely HTTPS-only, short enough that backing out costs
  a playbook run and five minutes -- and stage 2, max-age=31536000, is a commit
  of its own. What has to be true before that commit is made is written down in
  docs/issues/060-nginx-sends-no-strict-transport-security-header.rst, and the
  deploy that carries either stage is in README.rst under "The security
  maintenance window".

  Two directives are deliberately absent, as rulings rather than omissions;
  060 argues both. `includeSubDomains` would commit every future name under
  each of these three, and there is no such name today -- the three sites are
  siblings, so none of them is covered by another's header in any case.
  `preload` is irreversible in practice and is a promise about the whole of
  ambitone.com, which is not this project's to make.

  `always` is there so nginx's own error responses carry the header too: a 502
  while uWSGI restarts is exactly a moment at which a browser should stay
  pinned. One add_header on the server is enough only while no location block
  adds one of its own -- nginx drops every inherited add_header from a block
  that does -- so a location that ever grows a header has to repeat this line.
  None of the ones below has one.
-#}
upstream django {
    server unix:/{{ uwsgi_socket }};
}

{% for server in item.value.servers %}
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name {{ server.domain }};
    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/{{ server.domain }}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/{{ server.domain }}/privkey.pem;

    # HSTS stage 1 of two (issue 060): five minutes, not a year. This promise
    # cannot be withdrawn faster than it expires, and it makes the certbot
    # renewal cron load-bearing for the site opening at all -- a certificate
    # that silently fails to renew stops being a warning a visitor can click
    # through. Five minutes is the value you can back out of. Advancing it to
    # 31536000 is a deliberate commit against issue 060, which says what has to
    # be true first; the reasoning, includeSubDomains and preload included, is
    # in the template this file is rendered from.
    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300" always;

{% for path, alias in (server.static_paths|default({})).items() %}
    location {{ path }} {
        alias {{ alias }};
    }
{% endfor %}

{% if server.app_path is defined %}
    location {{ server.app_path }} {
        uwsgi_pass django;
        include    /etc/nginx/uwsgi_params;
    }
{% endif %}
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name {{ server.domain }};
    # No Strict-Transport-Security here, deliberately: a browser is required to
    # ignore it on a cleartext response, and whoever could answer this port
    # instead of nginx could send it just as easily.
    return 301 https://{{ server.domain }}$request_uri;
}
{% endfor %}